WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.850
RoboFailAtlas turns diverse robot failures into structured supervision for reward learning and policy improvement.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:10.038
The dataset covers eighty five tasks.

00:00:10.038 --> 00:00:14.850
Its structured subset contains twenty nine thousand five hundred trajectories.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.919
Frame intervals link actions, visual evidence, and structured labels.

00:00:18.919 --> 00:00:21.850
Recovery and terminal success are recorded separately.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:28.675
The taxonomy covers seventy actions, thirty six failure types, fifteen outcomes, and three recovery states.

00:00:28.675 --> 00:00:30.850
Uncertain annotation fields are masked.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:36.540
Here, the bottle is released at an unstable angle and falls onto its side.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.850
The required upright orientation remains unmet.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:43.700
In another rollout, a failed block grasp is recovered by a later regrasp.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.850
Local recovery remains separate from the final trajectory outcome.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:51.837
Contact can collapse a block stack, while repeated attempts can leave a fan unrecovered.

00:00:51.837 --> 00:00:54.850
Process errors and unmet task conditions have separate labels.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:58.075
Task instructions and observations enter a shared model.

00:00:58.075 --> 00:01:02.097
Joint heads predict task and failure signals, trained with masked losses.

00:01:02.097 --> 00:01:06.850
During PPO, the reward model stays frozen while its feedback updates the policy.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.840
These replays use the same scored frames as the paper.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.062
During pot lifting, predicted progress falls after grip loss.

00:01:16.062 --> 00:01:19.814
Markers are saved scores; lines connect neighboring samples.

00:01:19.814 --> 00:01:26.850
A later retry eventually succeeds in the microwave rollout, and the final progress score rises.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:38.850
Across five RoboTwin tasks, structured rewards achieve fifty point six percent average success, compared with forty point eight for environment rewards, using the same one hundred evaluation scenes per task.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:46.850
Failure guided selection improves success from thirty nine point six to forty six point two percent.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:56.850
On two real robot tasks, corrective demonstrations improve average success by seven percentage points over additional successful demonstrations.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.850
